Warlord's Lore
The Starchild's Lore
The Starchild was chosen by the stars themselves, her heart bound to the Eternal Core. With its radiance she bore the gifts of her people — shields of dawn, wings of light, and the forbidden art to stir the hearts of suns. To the Sun Civilization she was both guardian and flame eternal, but beneath the divine radiance lived a woman whose judgment would shape the fate of empires.
She had always prided herself on her ability to see truth, to illuminate deception with the light of certainty. When the mage council brought her evidence of the Archmage's betrayal—documents signed in his hand, communications with Void agents, research that had clearly crossed into corruption—she believed them without question. Why wouldn't she? They were her advisors, her trusted counselors.
When she arrived to find them dead by the Archmage's power, her grief turned instantly to rage. He tried to explain, tried to claim he was framed, but she saw only a traitor making excuses. Her Wrath of the Sun tore through his defenses. Her Celestial Nova burned away his lies. When he fell, broken and dying, she felt only grim satisfaction. Justice had been served.
Then came the Great Betrayal in full force, and she understood too late that she had been played. The Null Hour's manipulations had used her own certainty against her, turned her righteous fury into a weapon that destroyed one of her greatest defenders. Thousands died in the chaos that followed. Nightblade, her closest friend, took a lethal blow meant for her—and when he survived, she rewarded his sacrifice with accusation and exile.
The fabricated evidence was perfect. Communications between Nightblade and the Archmage. Authorization documents. A pattern that seemed undeniable. And she, so certain of her ability to see truth, believed it all. When he protested his innocence, she heard only the desperate lies of a co-conspirator. When he refused to leave quietly, she had him driven out with violence.
The weight of those choices crushes her every day. She destroyed the Archmage, who was trying to help them. She exiled Nightblade, who literally died to protect her. And because of her mistakes, the Great Betrayal succeeded beyond The Null Hour's wildest expectations.
"I should have listened," she whispers to The Dawnbreaker in quiet moments. "I should have questioned. I who illuminate all shadows, and I cast out the light itself because I was so certain I was right."
When Nightblade returned and massacred her people, she watched The Dawnbreaker fight him, heard their exchange of words. Part of her wanted to scream the truth—that she had been wrong, that he had been innocent, that she had destroyed him for nothing. But shame held her silent. How could she face him after what she'd done?
Now The Starchild carries a burden heavier than any weapon. She fights harder, burns brighter, pushes herself beyond all limits—not to be a hero, but to atone. She created the Attack Moon in desperate alliance with the Paradox Empress, hoping that overwhelming power might correct her failures. But power alone cannot undo the past.
She still believes in the light. She still fights for her people. But her certainty has been replaced with doubt, her righteous judgment tempered by the knowledge of how catastrophically wrong she can be. And somewhere in the dark, her greatest mistake walks as an assassin, and her paranoia destroyed a scholar who only wanted to help.
Until she finds a way to make this right, she will carry that flame—not as a beacon of certainty, but as a penance for the darkness she herself created.

The Dawnbreaker's Lore
The Dawnbreaker's path was not born of divine decree but forged in the harsh light of discipline. From the moment she could stand, her body was a canvas for the martial arts, each movement a prayer to the sun. Her peers fell away, unable to match her relentless pursuit of perfection, a mastery so profound that every fiber of her being obeyed her conscious will, and her form became a perfect instrument for her purpose.
She was there during the Great Betrayal, fighting alongside Nightblade when the Void forces struck. She saw him throw himself into the lethal beam, saw his body nearly torn apart to save The Starchild. She helped carry him to the medical ward, sat vigil as the healers worked to save him.
And she was there when The Starchild arrived with accusations of betrayal, when everything fell apart.
"This is insane," The Dawnbreaker had protested. "He took a killing blow for you. How can you possibly think he's a traitor?"
"The evidence is clear," The Starchild replied, her voice cold. "Sometimes the most loyal act is the deepest cover."
The Dawnbreaker didn't want to believe it. But the documents seemed real, the pattern seemed undeniable, and The Starchild's certainty was absolute. When ordered to help drive Nightblade out, she obeyed, though every strike felt like a betrayal of everything they'd trained for together.
Months later, when he returned and slaughtered her people, she fought him in the crystal halls. Their battle shook the citadel's foundations, her perfect martial forms against his flawless technique. But as they fought, she spoke to him, desperate to understand.
"Why?" she demanded, parrying his strikes. "You saved her. You nearly died for her. Why would you do this?"
His response was bitter and cold. "Ask your Starchild what really happened."
For the briefest moment, his assault faltered. Behind his cold demeanor, she saw something that looked like pain. "I protected her," he said quietly. "And she destroyed me for it."
The words hit harder than any blade. When she finally forced him to retreat, bloodied but unbroken, she saw him pause at the threshold. He touched the white sheath at his side with something that might have been reverence, then looked back at her with eyes full of accusation.
After he vanished, she went to The Starchild and demanded the truth. What she learned shattered something fundamental in her understanding of the world. The fabricated evidence. The Null Hour's manipulation. The Archmage's innocence. Nightblade's sacrifice turned into punishment.
Now The Dawnbreaker trains harder than ever, but not just for strength. She trains to be better than blind obedience, to question even when certainty seems absolute. She knows that somewhere beneath the Void's corrupted weapon lies the soul of a warrior who chose to sacrifice everything for light—and was rewarded with exile and hatred.
She prepares for the day when the truth might reach him, when he might remember not just what happened, but what should have happened. And when that day comes, she will stand beside him against anyone—even The Starchild herself if necessary. Because she failed him once by following orders. She won't make that mistake again.

Attack Moon's Lore
It is a legend whispered only in the deepest archives of the Sun Civilization, a relic of desperate alliance forged in the fires of the Great Betrayal. The Attack Moon is not a person, but a living construct, a sentient fragment of a star bound in ancient, impenetrable armor. It was built as retaliation, a weapon of such power that it could bring judgment to those who had wounded the light itself.
When the Void Archmage's betrayal devastated the Lightborne forces and claimed Nightblade, The Starchild knew conventional warfare would not suffice. In her darkest hour, she reached out to the enigmatic Paradox Faction, seeking their impossible technology. The Quantum Empress, moved by ancient bonds of alliance, provided the paradoxical engineering needed to create something that existed both as weapon and fortress, both real and impossible.
The Attack Moon's descent upon the Void was like a falling star carrying the wrath of creation itself. Its impossible weapons, forged from both stellar fire and paradoxical physics, brought devastation to the void-touched worlds. Yet even this ultimate weapon could not achieve total victory—the combined might of the Void Archmage, The Null Hour, and their dark legions managed to wound it and force its retreat.
Now the Attack Moon waits in the depths of space, its consciousness holding memories of battles fought across realities, its guns trained on distant stars. It is both monument to alliance between Light and Paradox, and testament to the terrible cost of betrayal. When it next awakens, the very fabric of space will tremble at its judgment.

God Engine Lore:
Solar Colossus  (Sunborn God Machine)
Forged upon the desert forges of Erythra, the Solar Colossus was carved from the buried citadels of glass and bound with the Ember of Dawn. Its core houses the Sunborn’s shard of the Silver Key, which fuels an eternal furnace.
Its mere presence scorches the battlefield; armies ignite like offerings, and night turns to endless noon.
The Necronomium fragment woven into its design describes “The Flame that Burns Beyond Dawn”, and so the Solar Colossus is revered as the Sunborn’s proof of divine succession.
